1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,835
Section two taught you to read a benchmark table critically.

2
00:00:03,835 --> 00:00:08,481
Here's what replaces it when an actual decision is on the table: your own golden

3
00:00:08,481 --> 00:00:13,243
set's pass rate, your own cost model's number, and a latency figure, side by side,

4
00:00:13,243 --> 00:00:15,857
for every model you're genuinely considering.

5
00:00:15,857 --> 00:00:20,619
Skepticism about benchmarks was always meant to lead somewhere, not just leave you

6
00:00:20,619 --> 00:00:22,710
doubting every number you're handed.

7
00:00:22,710 --> 00:00:27,588
This is where it leads: build the table yourself, from your own evidence, instead of

8
00:00:27,588 --> 00:00:31,363
distrusting someone else's and having nothing to replace it with.

9
00:00:31,363 --> 00:00:35,603
A public benchmark tells you how a model performs on someone else's task.

10
00:00:35,603 --> 00:00:40,307
Your scorecard tells you how it performs on yours, at what it actually costs you,

11
00:00:40,307 --> 00:00:42,630
and how long a real user actually waits.

12
00:00:42,630 --> 00:00:47,044
That's the document worth building before anyone commits to a model, not the

13
00:00:47,044 --> 00:00:49,948
leaderboard screenshot from the announcement post.

14
00:00:49,948 --> 00:00:54,826
This is the natural endpoint of three separate threads this course has been building

15
00:00:54,826 --> 00:00:59,705
since Section two: reading a benchmark critically, so you know what it isn't telling

16
00:00:59,705 --> 00:01:04,467
you; building a golden set, so you have something better to score against; and the

17
00:01:04,467 --> 00:01:09,287
cost model from two lectures ago, so price sits on the same page as quality instead

18
00:01:09,287 --> 00:01:11,378
of arriving as a surprise afterward.

19
00:01:11,928 --> 00:01:16,747
Read this table in a specific order, because reading it left to right, model by

20
00:01:16,747 --> 00:01:20,102
model, is how teams talk themselves into the wrong row.

21
00:01:20,102 --> 00:01:24,860
Read the pass rate column first, alone, against whatever floor Section three's

22
00:01:24,860 --> 00:01:27,910
acceptance criteria actually set for this feature.

23
00:01:27,910 --> 00:01:32,912
If that floor is eighty-five percent, the budget model is gone before its price or

24
00:01:32,912 --> 00:01:34,376
its speed matter at all.

25
00:01:34,376 --> 00:01:39,012
Seventy-one percent isn't a cheaper, slower version of an acceptable answer.

26
00:01:39,012 --> 00:01:43,892
It's a model that fails the threshold, and no amount of savings on the other two

27
00:01:43,892 --> 00:01:45,173
columns changes that.

28
00:01:45,173 --> 00:01:49,626
Notice, too, that the pass rate in this table isn't a number you look up.

29
00:01:49,626 --> 00:01:54,567
It's the same fifty-case golden set from Section four, the same rubric, run three

30
00:01:54,567 --> 00:01:58,776
separate times, once per candidate model, under identical conditions.

31
00:01:58,776 --> 00:02:03,351
Nothing else in this table means anything if that first column was measured

32
00:02:03,351 --> 00:02:05,059
inconsistently between rows.

33
00:02:05,059 --> 00:02:09,695
Identical conditions is doing more work in that sentence than it looks like.

34
00:02:09,695 --> 00:02:14,758
Same prompt, same cases, same rubric, same judge, whether that judge is a person or

35
00:02:14,758 --> 00:02:17,564
the calibrated LLM-as-judge from Section four.

36
00:02:17,564 --> 00:02:22,139
Change any one of those between rows and the pass rate column stops being a

37
00:02:22,139 --> 00:02:26,775
comparison and becomes three unrelated numbers that happen to share a table.

38
00:02:27,325 --> 00:02:32,185
Notice what the floor actually buys you: it turns a three-way comparison into a

39
00:02:32,185 --> 00:02:36,614
two-way one, and a cleaner argument in the room where this gets decided.

40
00:02:36,614 --> 00:02:41,597
Once the budget model is out, the real question is this: do flagship's five extra

41
00:02:41,597 --> 00:02:46,211
points of pass rate justify four times the cost and double the latency over

42
00:02:46,211 --> 00:02:46,764
mid-tier?

43
00:02:46,764 --> 00:02:51,932
That's a genuine trade-off, worth an actual conversation, and a completely different

44
00:02:51,932 --> 00:02:56,915
one from accidentally comparing a disqualified model against two that cleared the

45
00:02:56,915 --> 00:02:57,161
bar.

46
00:02:57,161 --> 00:03:02,267
There's no universal right answer to that trade-off, and this lecture won't pretend

47
00:03:02,267 --> 00:03:03,066
there is one.

48
00:03:03,066 --> 00:03:08,049
A feature where a wrong answer is expensive, the kind of blast radius Section six

49
00:03:08,049 --> 00:03:12,356
spent a whole lecture naming, leans toward paying for the five points.

50
00:03:12,356 --> 00:03:17,216
A feature where speed is the actual product experience, and both models already

51
00:03:17,216 --> 00:03:20,107
clear the bar comfortably, leans the other way.

52
00:03:20,107 --> 00:03:24,721
The scorecard's job is making that a visible choice, not making it for you.

53
00:03:24,721 --> 00:03:29,273
Write the reasoning down alongside the choice, not just the choice itself.

54
00:03:29,273 --> 00:03:34,256
"We picked flagship because the failure cost outweighs four times the spend" is a

55
00:03:34,256 --> 00:03:37,762
decision someone can revisit intelligently in six months.

56
00:03:37,762 --> 00:03:42,561
"We picked flagship" on its own is a decision nobody can argue with or defend,

57
00:03:42,561 --> 00:03:47,605
because the actual reasoning behind it left the room the moment the meeting ended.

58
00:03:48,155 --> 00:03:52,969
Say the honest caveat, because a scorecard built once and treated as permanent

59
00:03:52,969 --> 00:03:57,536
quietly goes stale in exactly the way a corpus does if nobody revisits it.

60
00:03:57,536 --> 00:03:58,523
Pricing changes.

61
00:03:58,523 --> 00:03:59,511
New models ship.

62
00:03:59,511 --> 00:04:04,571
The mid-tier option that won this quarter's comparison might be beaten outright by

63
00:04:04,571 --> 00:04:09,570
something released next month, at a lower price, that didn't exist when you built

64
00:04:09,570 --> 00:04:10,249
this table.

65
00:04:10,249 --> 00:04:15,186
Put a date on every scorecard, and a reminder to rerun it, because the model you

66
00:04:15,186 --> 00:04:19,568
locked in eight months ago is not guaranteed to still be the right row.

67
00:04:19,568 --> 00:04:24,752
This is also the argument for keeping your golden set genuinely reusable rather than

68
00:04:24,752 --> 00:04:25,986
a one-time artifact.

69
00:04:25,986 --> 00:04:31,109
Rerunning the scorecard against a new model release should take an afternoon, not a

70
00:04:31,109 --> 00:04:36,293
fresh round of case selection, because the fifty cases and the rubric already exist.

71
00:04:36,293 --> 00:04:41,106
Treat the golden set as permanent infrastructure and the recheck becomes cheap

72
00:04:41,106 --> 00:04:43,205
enough that people actually do it.

73
00:04:43,755 --> 00:04:48,453
That's the document worth building, and the definition's last clause does the real

74
00:04:48,453 --> 00:04:52,291
work: below the floor, removed, before anything else gets compared.

75
00:04:52,291 --> 00:04:55,213
Try it on a real decision if one's in front of you.

76
00:04:55,213 --> 00:04:59,567
Most people building this for the first time discover they've been comparing

77
00:04:59,567 --> 00:05:04,150
benchmark numbers for one model against a gut feeling for another, which isn't a

78
00:05:04,150 --> 00:05:08,733
comparison at all, just two different kinds of guess sitting next to each other.

79
00:05:08,733 --> 00:05:10,280
The fix is rarely dramatic.

80
00:05:10,280 --> 00:05:14,864
It's usually one afternoon spent running the same fifty cases through the second

81
00:05:14,864 --> 00:05:19,160
model, so the comparison is finally apples to apples instead of a benchmark

82
00:05:19,160 --> 00:05:22,426
screenshot against a vague impression from using it once.

83
00:05:22,976 --> 00:05:24,753
One thing before you move on.

84
00:05:24,753 --> 00:05:29,534
Download the template and confirm the floor first, in writing, before any cost

85
00:05:29,534 --> 00:05:31,066
number goes in the sheet.

86
00:05:31,066 --> 00:05:36,091
Write it down even if it feels obvious, because "obvious" thresholds have a way of

87
00:05:36,091 --> 00:05:41,178
quietly shifting once a cheap, fast option is sitting right there in the same table

88
00:05:41,178 --> 00:05:42,220
looking tempting.

89
00:05:42,220 --> 00:05:47,246
Then run at least two real candidates through it, using your own golden set, not a

90
00:05:47,246 --> 00:05:47,981
leaderboard.

91
00:05:47,981 --> 00:05:53,068
Next lecture is the margin trap: what happens when a model clears every row of this

92
00:05:53,068 --> 00:05:57,910
scorecard and the feature still loses money per user, because the scorecard was

93
00:05:57,910 --> 00:05:59,258
never the whole story.
